The task is to decide, for a point p and a triangle with vertices a, b, c, whether p is inside the triangle or on one of its edges, returning False when it lies outside.

The two consistent answers are these: p lies inside or on the boundary of the triangle exactly when it lies on the same side of every edge as the opposite vertex, or when the point coincides with any of the triangle's vertices.

My helper code uses the cross product. The cross product of two two-dimensional vectors (x, y) and (u, v) is xu - yv, which gives twice the signed area of the triangle formed by those vectors. For a triangle's edge from q to r, the sign of the cross product of (r - q) with (p - q) tells which side of the edge the point lies on: negative means it is on the side that holds the opposite vertex, and non-negative means it is on the other side or on the line through q and r. So a point inside or on the boundary of the triangle has a non-positive cross product with each of the three edges it forms with the other two vertices. Special care is needed for a repeated vertex such as two corners at the origin: the edge from that vertex to itself has zero length, and the cross product is always zero, so the zero side side plays no role in the comparison and the point on the repeated vertex is accepted. Here is the implementation:

    def point_in_triangle(p, a, b, c):
        def cross(q, r):
            return (r[0] - q[0]) * (p[1] - q[1]) - (r[1] - q[1]) * (p[0] - q[0])
        c1 = cross(a, b)
        c2 = cross(b, c)
        c3 = cross(c, a)
        if c1 < 0 and c2 < 0 and c3 < 0:
            return False
        if p == a or p == b or p == c:
            return True
        return any(c >= 0 for c in (c1, c2, c3))

